{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01AAVbTrSJKJn5fTn54Amvk9","type":"tool_result","content":"180\t  property; it does **not** excuse skipping threading code in\n181\t  the C body.\n182\t* **Cross-thread wake-up** (C uses `pipe()` + `write(1)` to wake\n183\t  a `select(2)`-blocked loop) → `std::sync::mpsc`,\n184\t  `std::sync::Condvar`, or a self-pipe via `nix`.  Real bodies.\n185\t* **Event loops (`picoquic_packet_loop_v3` etc.)** are the heart\n186\t  of the library.  Translate the receive-process-send loop\n187\t  directly.\n188\t* **`select(2)` / `poll(2)` / `io_uring`** → use the `mio` crate\n189\t  (or `tokio` lower-level bits) for portable poll, or\n190\t  platform-specific `nix` bindings.  Real Rust code.\n191\t* **Loglib functions (binlog / qlog / textlog / performance_log\n192\t  / logger).**  Tests call them; translate them.  Use the\n193\t  `log` crate plus standard file I/O.\n194\t* **Sub-system not yet translated** is never a valid blocker.\n195\t  Translate the sub-system **first**, then come back.  The whole\n196\t  library has to be translated; do it in dependency order.\n197\t\n198\t### What to do instead when a body is hard\n199\t\n200\t1. **Translate the helpers it needs first.**  A function that\n201\t   \"needs `picoquic_get_stored_ticket`\" really means \"translate\n202\t   `picoquic_get_stored_ticket` first.\"  That helper is the\n203\t   blocker; resolve it.\n204\t2. **Add the missing fields / types.**  If a C body reads\n205\t   `cnx->nb_zero_rtt_sent` and the Rust struct doesn't have it,\n206\t   **add it** (it's already in `internal.rs` as\n207\t   `Connection::nb_zero_rtt_sent` — verify before assuming\n208\t   absence).\n209\t3. **Use Rust ownership idioms.**  \"Returns `&mut` from `&self`\""}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f8748440-59b5-4ac8-a898-54be0860e8a3","uuid":"557a8b6f-9d68-46c5-abc1-6a8fcad6d4a0","timestamp":"2026-05-06T12:49:45.021Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/richbarn/Projects/picoquic/xlate/impl_translation_guide.md","content":"  property; it does **not** excuse skipping threading code in\n  the C body.\n* **Cross-thread wake-up** (C uses `pipe()` + `write(1)` to wake\n  a `select(2)`-blocked loop) → `std::sync::mpsc`,\n  `std::sync::Condvar`, or a self-pipe via `nix`.  Real bodies.\n* **Event loops (`picoquic_packet_loop_v3` etc.)** are the heart\n  of the library.  Translate the receive-process-send loop\n  directly.\n* **`select(2)` / `poll(2)` / `io_uring`** → use the `mio` crate\n  (or `tokio` lower-level bits) for portable poll, or\n  platform-specific `nix` bindings.  Real Rust code.\n* **Loglib functions (binlog / qlog / textlog / performance_log\n  / logger).**  Tests call them; translate them.  Use the\n  `log` crate plus standard file I/O.\n* **Sub-system not yet translated** is never a valid blocker.\n  Translate the sub-system **first**, then come back.  The whole\n  library has to be translated; do it in dependency order.\n\n### What to do instead when a body is hard\n\n1. **Translate the helpers it needs first.**  A function that\n   \"needs `picoquic_get_stored_ticket`\" really means \"translate\n   `picoquic_get_stored_ticket` first.\"  That helper is the\n   blocker; resolve it.\n2. **Add the missing fields / types.**  If a C body reads\n   `cnx->nb_zero_rtt_sent` and the Rust struct doesn't have it,\n   **add it** (it's already in `internal.rs` as\n   `Connection::nb_zero_rtt_sent` — verify before assuming\n   absence).\n3. **Use Rust ownership idioms.**  \"Returns `&mut` from `&self`\"","numLines":30,"startLine":180,"totalLines":339}}}
